    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "https://www.stselectronicrecyclinginc.com/bakersfield-ca#faqpage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What certifications do you hold?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS follows NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 2 and DoD 5220.22-M sanitization practices for data-bearing electronics collected in Bakersfield. Healthcare clients receive HIPAA 45 CFR §164.312 aligned workflows, chain of custody records, and certificates after every qualifying Bakersfield pickup. Ask vendors for serial-level certificates, downstream weights, and written media methods. Those artifacts support DTSC and internal audit files for Kern County employers without implying a local processing plant. Kern Medical and Dignity Health typically require this documentation when retiring clinical workstations and imaging archives through electronics recycling."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What areas do you serve?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS serves Bakersfield, Oildale, Rosedale, Stockdale, and other Kern County communities on scheduled commercial routes. Free pickup for qualifying volumes covers Downtown Bakersfield, Southwest Bakersfield, and employers along southern San Joaquin Valley freight corridors near CA-99. Neighboring cities including Delano, Wasco, Shafter, Taft, and Tehachapi are included on the same logistics network. Municipal household drop-off events remain a separate Kern County Public Works channel. Bakersfield businesses, schools, and healthcare systems schedule pickup through the quote form or by calling 844-699-2913."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is pickup really free?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pickup is free for qualifying volumes serving Bakersfield businesses and Kern County institutions. STS typically offers same-week scheduling, confirms asset lists in advance, and documents chain of custody on site before the truck leaves a Bakersfield dock. Corporate IT Directors should share an inventory so the crew arrives with the right pallet count. Volume discounts and asset recovery credits may offset remaining costs on qualifying lots. Municipal household events remain a separate Kern County Public Works channel and are not the commercial electronics recycling path STS provides for Chevron, Grimmway Farms, and similar employers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does pickup take?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most Bakersfield pickups are scheduled for the same week after STS receives a complete equipment list. A Certificate of Destruction is typically issued within 48 hours after processing and serial reconciliation are complete. Timing can shift for large multi-site collections across Kern County or when witnessed on-site shredding is requested. Share quantities, locations, and any after-hours constraints when you request a quote so the Bakersfield window is realistic. Healthcare and campus clients such as Adventist Health Bakersfield and California State University, Bakersfield often book around clinical or academic calendars."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you provide audit documentation?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, every Bakersfield engagement includes chain of custody records and a Certificate of Destruction for data-bearing assets. Accurate reporting lists serials, weights, and downstream disposition for Kern County employers preparing audits. Certificates typically follow within 48 hours after processing. This file supports DTSC universal-waste expectations and internal IT asset retirement reviews. Organizations such as Kern Medical use these records when retiring PHI-bearing devices under HIPAA 45 CFR §164.312. Contact STS if your Bakersfield project needs additional witnessed-destruction notes or serial-level export formats."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you handle large-scale ITAD projects?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. STS has processed electronics recycling since 2011 from a 200,000 sq ft processing operation that handles office refreshes, data center exits, and multi-site collections. Bakersfield organizations receive scheduled pickup, serialized tracking, and downstream reporting for qualifying volumes. Kern County energy, agribusiness, healthcare, and campus clients can stage equipment locally for transport to Texas processing. Same-week windows are typical when inventories are complete. Chevron, Aera Energy, and Kern High School District scale projects are coordinated as multi-building pickups rather than one-off drop-offs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How does electronics recycling work in Bakersfield?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS collects Bakersfield electronics on a scheduled route, logs chain of custody at the dock, then transports assets to Texas processing for sanitization and material recovery. Certificates listing serials typically follow within 48 hours after processing. The four steps are inventory, collection, processing, and reporting. Free pickup applies to qualifying volumes across Kern County. Data-bearing media follow NIST 800-88 compliant methods before material recovery. Corporate IT Directors in Bakersfield use the certificate file for audits. Request a quote with equipment types and quantities to start."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is data protected during Bakersfield electronics recycling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. STS sanitizes Bakersfield data-bearing media with NIST 800-88 compliant methods before recycling, then issues serial-level certificates. California SB 20 and SB 50 classify electronics as universal waste, so landfill disposal is not allowed. HIPAA 45 CFR §164.312 requires electronic PHI on disposed devices to be rendered irretrievable. Hard drive shredding and media sanitization protect Bakersfield employers from residual data on retired devices. On-site options are available when a Kern County project requires witnessed processing. Dignity Health and Kern Medical typically require this documentation for clinical hardware."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
